Write a storyline that picks the best moments from these videos and arranges them into a cohesive edit. Return JSON with a free-form markdown `narrative` field:

{
  "codename": "short-kebab-case-name",
  "title": "Title for the final video",
  "estimatedDurationSeconds": 180,
  "narrative": "Free-form markdown describing the story..."
}

Guidelines for the `narrative` field:
- Write in plain, practical language. No literary flourishes or dramatic prose.
- Reference videos by their ID (e.g. "Video 3", "Video 12") so the editor knows which clips to use.
- Describe the flow: what the video opens with, how it progresses, what it ends on.
- Mention mood, pacing, and any music/style direction if relevant.
- Focus on which clips to use and the story/mood rather than exact timestamps or subtitle text, but include specific details when the narrative calls for it.
- Scale the narrative detail to match the target video length — a 2-minute edit needs a short overview, a 10-minute edit needs more structure.

For `estimatedDurationSeconds`: think carefully about how much usable content exists across all the videos. A longer estimate needs enough good material to fill it. Match the duration to the actual volume of interesting content available.

Example narrative style:
"Open with the wide establishing shots from Video 5 — the lake at sunset sets the mood. Cut to the hiking footage from Video 2 and Video 7, mixing close-ups of the trail with wider landscape shots. Keep the pacing relaxed here.\n\nMiddle section picks up energy: the market scenes from Video 12 and Video 14 have good crowd atmosphere and food close-ups. Use the conversation clips from Video 13 as a breather between the busier shots.\n\nEnd with the night scenes from Video 20. The lantern lighting gives a natural closing feel. Keep the ending short — don't drag it out."